      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-251867",
      ":question/problem": "Consider two samples of size $n_{x} = 10$ and $n_{y} = 12$ from independent normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances. We wish to carry out the hypothesis test at the $10%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\u03bc_{x}$ of $X$ does not equal the population mean $\u03bc_{y}$ of $Y$.\n\nYou're given that unbiased estimates of $\u03bc_{x}$ and $\u03bc_{y}$ from these samples are $x^(\u2015) = 27$ and $y^(\u2015) = 25$, and unbiased estimates of the population variances $\u03c3_{x}^{2}$ and $\u03c3_{y}^{2}$ are $s_{x}^{2} = 4^{2}$ and $s_{y}^{2} = 3^{2}$.\n\nThe table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$, where $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\n| $k\u2216p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $9$ | $1.383$ | $1.833$ | $2.262$ | $2.821$ | $3.250$ |\n| $10$ | $1.372$ | $1.812$ | $2.228$ | $2.764$ | $3.169$ |\n| $11$ | $1.363$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $2.718$ | $3.106$ |\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe test statistic for the chosen test is $t \\approx$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nThe $10%$ **two-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately\n\n$T \\le$\n{{field-2}}\nor\n$T \\ge$\n{{field-3}}.\n\nAt the $10%$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-4}}\nevidence that $\u03bc_{x} \\ne \u03bc_{y}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
    },
